  "query_preview": "..\nHigh pressure is in place and will be sticking around for the\nmost of the week. There is a surface high pressure in the Four\nCorners to New Mexico and a surface low pressure in the Gulf of\nCalifornia. This interaction has created a pressure gradient,\nresulting in easterly breezes in the last two days. Ensemble \nmodels continue to show this gradient through Monday with the\ntightest gradient by Sunday morning. The latest hi-res guidance\nhas highlighted breezy to windy conditions across Southeast\nArizona starting early in the morning and peaking late morning.\nREFS probability of gusts greater than 35 mph are 85% and \nhigher from the Marana to Nogales line and east. Locales on the \nthe lee side of mountains have 60-90% chances for gusts greater\nthan 45 mph. Therefore, we have issued a Wind Advisory for \nthose areas starting at 8am and ending at 2 pm MST. Easterly \nwinds will continue through Monday, but at lower intensity. \n\nHigh pressure will wobble a bit and tapping into some subtropical\nmoisture each day. This wobbling effect will shift the \nplacement of very low chances for rain showers and \nthunderstorms. These chances may be more of cloud build ups, \nespecially on the higher terrain.\n\nTowards the end of the new week, cluster analysis shows a\ntroughing pattern along the western coast. There is a large\ndisagreement with the ensemble members on the placement and\ntiming of the trough. Highest probability (37% of ensemble \nmembers) solution is a trough extending down from the Pacific \nNorthwest to California, bringing chances for rain showers by \nthe end of the weekend. The other 3 solutions have different \nplacement and extent of the trough, such one solution (22% of \nmembers) has a close low by northern California, potentially\ntapping into better moisture. More details will come as we get \nthrough the new week.",
